จในสวน", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/rong-jae/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/rong-jae.webp", "description": "สถานที่ที่เอกสารเชื่อมกับประเพณีจีนและการสืบทอดวัฒนธรรม", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "streetAddress": "ซอยโรงพระ ถนนราษฎร์บำรุง อำเภอตะกั่วป่า จังหวัดพังงา", "addressLocality": "ตะกั่วป่า", "addressRegion": "พังงา", "addressCountry": "TH"}}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "สถานที่นี้มีประวัติความเป็นมาอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "เอกสารแผนการดำเนินงานด้านสิ่งแวดล้อมของเทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่าระบุว่าโรงเจในสวนมีชื่อว่าศาลเจ้าเซ้งเหล่งเก้ง และมีอายุนับร้อยปี โดยไม่ได้ระบุปีสร้างที่แน่นอน เทศบาลระบุผู้ก่อตั้งคือนายส้อ เทียนเส้ง ซึ่งเป็นชาวจีนโพ้นทะเล"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "มีจุดเด่นอะไรให้ชม", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "องค์เทพที่เทศบาลระบุว่าประดิษฐานในศาลเจ้า มีอีกชื่อว่าเจ้าพ่อหยาดน้ำค้าง และมีประวัติอัญเชิญมาจากประเทศจีน บล็อกหรอยจังรายงานว่าอาม่าตั๋นซี่หลุนขายขนมโกสุ้ยที่โรงเจในสวนช่วงเทศกาลกินเจ เป็นข้อมูลจากบล็อกที่ยังต้องตรวจสอบการจำหน่ายในปีที่จะเดินทาง"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "เอกสารเทศบาลกล่าวถึงที่นี่อย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "สถานที่ที่เอกสารเชื่อมกับประเพณีจีนและการสืบทอดวัฒนธรรม"}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/rong-jae/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/rong-jae/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/rong-jae/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/rong-jae/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Shrines & Vegetarian Halls</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Shrines and vegetarian halls</span><h1>The Shrine in the Garden</h1><p class="english">The Shrine in the Garden</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">6 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="rong-jae">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/rong-jae.webp" alt="โรงเจในสวน" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>An old shrine of the Chinese community in Takua Pa</h3><p class="era-years">The exact construction year is unknown; municipal documents state that it is more than a century old</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Rong Jae Nai Suan, or Seng Leng Keng Shrine, appears in Takua Pa Town Municipality documents as a centuries-old shrine, but the same documents do not give its construction year. It is therefore not yet possible to place its beginnings in a particular reign.</p><p>The founder identified in municipal documents is Mr So Thian Seng, an overseas Chinese settler. After the shrine was completed, Phra Cheng Chui Cho Sue, or Chao Pho Yat Nam Khang, was brought from China and enshrined there. The place therefore likely served both as a site of worship and as a centre of belief for the Chinese community in the mining and port town of Takua Pa. However, there is no site-specific evidence for its construction year, the founder's policy rationale, or its everyday role at the time.</p><p>Another local document says that Mr So Seng had worked with Ku Chai Tung Shrine before opening the shrine in the garden. However, it cannot yet be confirmed that he was the same person as Mr So Thian Seng, so these are not presented as the same fact.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality): Environmental Operation Plan</a> · <a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Takua Pa Vegetarian Festival, สภาวัฒนธรรมตำบลตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Sub-district Cultural Council)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>A transition period with blank documents</h3><p class="era-years">No confirmed year or event has been found</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>Among the verifiable sources, no evidence has been found that the shrine changed ownership, was abandoned, or was restored in any particular year. No site-specific evidence has been found showing how the 2004 tsunami (พ.ศ. 2547) affected this building.</p><p>No historic-site registration entry has been found for Rong Jae Nai Suan, and there is no information that it was converted to another business or type of public building. The verifiable information for the intervening period is therefore limited to the continuity of the place name and memories of an old shrine. The history of another shrine in the old town should not be used to fill the gaps.</p><p>Takua Pa's context changed from a mining and maritime-trading town into a historic and cultural town, but the sources found do not yet connect this city-level change with specific events at Rong Jae Nai Suan.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality): Environmental Operation Plan</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>Still named in the Vegetarian Festival landscape, but operational status is unclear</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>Currently, verifiable online sources do not confirm who manages the shrine, whether it is open to visitors every day, what condition the building is in, or whether any restoration project is under way. It should therefore not be described as an open tourist attraction or as a place that can be visited without an appointment.</p><p>The contemporary evidence found is a review article reporting the sale of ko sui sweets at Rong Jae Nai Suan during the Vegetarian Festival. This indicates that the place was still used for a community festival activity, but it is a blog report, not a shrine schedule announcement, and does not confirm that the activity is held every year.</p><p>Travellers should use the Soi Rong Phra, Thanon Rat Bamrung address as a starting point and check with local people or the municipality before travelling, because no verified coordinates, opening hours, telephone number, or official contact channel are available.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roi Jang: Ko Sui, Takua Pa local dessert</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Takua Pa Town Municipality: Place Information Document</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Founding of the shrine</h3><p>The municipality document identified the founder as Mr. So Thian Seng, an overseas Chinese, but did not specify the founding year.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Inviting the deity from China</h3><p>After building the shrine, Phra Cheng Chui Cho Sue, or the Dewdrop God, was invited from China to be enshrined.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Kosui snack activity in the Vegetarian Festival</h3><p>A review article reported the selling of Kosui snacks at the Shrine in the Garden, but it is not an official announcement and it is not verified if it will be held every year.</p><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Deity Cheng Chui Cho Sue</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The deity that the municipality states is enshrined in the shrine. Also known as the Dewdrop Deity, it has a history of being invited from China.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Ko Sui snacks during the Vegetarian Festival</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The Roi Jang blog reports that Grandma Tan Si Lun sells Ko Sui snacks at the Shrine in the Garden during the Vegetarian Festival. This is information from a blog that still needs to be verified for sales in the year of travel.</p><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The name "Rong Chae Nai Suan" (Shrine in the Garden) is a common name for Seng Leng Keng Shrine, according to Takua Pa Town Municipality data.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Municipal documents mention a reputation for traditional Chinese healing. This is the history of the place's beliefs, not evidence of medical efficacy.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/guan-yu/">Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/pun-thao/">Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/kue-chai/">Kue Chai Tung Shrine</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>Takua Pa Town Municipality's environmental action plan documents say that Rong Jae Nai Suan is called Seng Leng Keng Shrine and is more than a century old, without specifying an exact construction year. The municipality identifies its founder as Mr So Thian Seng, an overseas Chinese settler.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>The deity that the municipality says is enshrined at the shrine is also known as Chao Pho Yat Nam Khang and is said to have been brought from China. The Hroi Jang blog reports that Ama Tan Si Lun sells ko sui sweets at Rong Jae Nai Suan during the Vegetarian Festival. This is blog information, and sales in the year of travel still need to be checked.</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>A place that documents connect with Chinese traditions and cultural continuity</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Town Municipality · Environmental Action Plan for Fiscal Year 2020 (พ.ศ. 2563)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Vegetarian Festival, Takua Pa Subdistrict Cultural Council · Copy on AnyFlip</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Hroi Jang Phang Nga · Ko Sui, a local sweet from Takua Pa, Phang Nga</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality): Environmental Operation Plan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Vegetarian Festival, สภาวัฒนธรรมตำบลตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Sub-district Cultural Council)</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://anyflip.com/tshcu/hnol/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Roi Jang: Ko Sui, Takua Pa local dessert</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://roijang.com/phangnga/โกสุ้ย-ขนมพื้นเมือ-งตะกั่วป่า-พังงา-2023-12-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The English name is used according to the brief. No source confirming this as the official English name has been found yet; SEO keywords are suggestions for searching, not search volume data.</li><li>No coordinates that can verify its identity have been found from Google Maps, OpenStreetMap, Mapcarta, or Wikidata.</li><li>A Google Map embedded in the Roi Jang article shows the name Rong Than Shrine Hiap Thian Tai Te, Thung Yai Shrine, Phang Nga Province, which does not match the Shrine in the Garden, so the coordinates from that map are not used.</li><li>The address is only known as Soi Rong Phra, Rat Bamrung Road; no specific building number, subdistrict, and postal code confirmed for the location have been found.</li><li>No verified opening hours and admission fees have been found, so it is not assumed that it is open every day or free.</li><li>No verified phone number, Facebook page, website, or LINE account for the shrine has been found.</li><li>No direct travel information to the shrine from Takua Pa town center, Phuket, or Khao Lak, including distances and public transport, has been found.</li><li>No exact construction year or restoration year has been found. The phrase "century-old" is not enough to calculate the construction year.</li><li>No specific evidence regarding the role of the Shrine in the Garden in the tin mining business has been found.</li><li>No verifiable description of this building's architecture has been found, so it is not stated as Sino-Portuguese or detailed with Chinese decorations.</li><li>No ancient monument registration announcement specific to the Shrine in the Garden has been found.</li><li>Founder information in the book on AnyFlip uses the name So Seng, while the municipality uses So Thian Seng, and a connection between the individuals cannot yet be made.</li><li>Only two highlights with direct evidence to the location were found, and the snack item is seasonal data from a blog. Therefore, it is not filled up to 3-6 items without evidence.</li><li>No month and lunar calendar specific to this shrine have been found, nor the 2026 schedule.</li><li>No visiting duration, dress code, parking, restrooms, ramps, or accessibility information for the elderly and wheelchairs have been found.</li><li>No verified walking routes from the shrine to nearby places have been found.</li><li>No official image link verifying it is the Shrine in the Garden and whose image can be checked has been found.</li><li>The newly referenced municipal document is the Environmental Operations Plan for Fiscal Year 2020.</li><li>No verified construction year, restoration year, or founding date has been found.</li><li>No evidence of changing hands, abandonment, other uses, effects from the 2004 tsunami, or site-specific ancient monument registration has been found.</li><li>No current caretaker, opening hours, building condition, development projects, coordinates, phone number, and official contact channels have been found.</li><li>No old or current photos that verify the identity of the Shrine in the Garden from readable sources have been found.</li><li>No news from the past five years specific to the Shrine in the Garden has been found.</li><li>The context of the tin mining era, maritime trade, and the Chinese community of Takua Pa still has no evidence tying it directly to specific events at this location.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\places\wat-boromthat\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet · Wat Lum Takuapa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Observing the town through its pagoda and the faith of its people · Khiri Khet Temple · Takuapa old town temple · Phang Nga temple"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet · Wat Lum Takuapa"><meta property="og:description" content="Observing the town through its pagoda and the faith of its people · Khiri Khet Temple · Takuapa old town temple · Phang Nga temple"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-boromthat/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-boromthat.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-boromthat.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-boromthat/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "วัดบรมธาตุคีรีเขต (วัดลุ่ม) ตะกั่วป่า", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-boromthat/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "TouristAttraction", "name": "วัดบรมธาตุคีรีเขต (วัดลุ่ม)", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-boromthat/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/wat-boromthat.webp", "description": "มีพระบรมสารีริกธาตุประดิษฐานอยู่ และเป็นสถานที่สำคัญทางศรัทธา", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "streetAddress": "ถนนกลั่นแก้ว ย่านตลาดเก่า (บริเวณซอยคีรีเขต)", "addressLocality": "ตะกั่วป่า", "addressRegion": "พังงา", "postalCode": "82110", "addressCountry": "TH"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 8.8336680169, "longitude": 98.3563150443}}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "เปิดให้เข้าชมเวลาใด", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "ข้อมูลยังไม่ยืนยัน · ทุกวัน 08.00 ถึง 17.00 น."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "มีค่าเข้าชมหรือไม่", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "ข้อมูลยังไม่ยืนยัน · ไม่เสียค่าเข้าชม"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "ควรแต่งกายอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "แต่งกายสุภาพเมื่อเข้าสักการะศาสนสถาน"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "เดินทางจากตัวเมืองตะกั่วป่าอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "มุ่งสู่ย่านเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า แล้วเข้าถนนกลั่นแก้ว/ซอยคีรีเขต"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "สถานที่นี้มีประวัติความเป็นมาอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "เดิมเป็นที่พักสงฆ์ของพระฝ่ายอรัญวาสี และมีอายุประมาณ 200 ปี ชาวบ้านเรียกวัดนี้ว่า ‘วัดลุ่ม’ เพราะตั้งอยู่ในพื้นที่ลาดลุ่ม"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "มีจุดเด่นอะไรให้ชม", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "ฐานเจดีย์โบราณที่มีต้นไทรใหญ่ขึ้นอยู่บนฐาน เป็นศูนย์รวมศรัทธาสำคัญของชุมชนตามข้อมูลเทศบาล ประดิษฐานอยู่ภายในวัด"}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-boromthat/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-boromthat/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-boromthat/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-boromthat/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</h1><p class="english">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">8 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-boromthat">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-boromthat.webp" alt="วัดบรมธาตุคีรีเขต (วัดลุ่ม)" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8336680169, 98.3563150443 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8336680169,98.3563150443" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd>Every day 08:00 to 17:00 <span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd>No admission fee <span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd>Dress modestly when visiting religious sites <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://www.lovethailand.org/travel/th/68-%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%B2/7959-%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%95-%28%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B8%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A1%29.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>From a forest monks' retreat to a town's sanctuary</h3><p class="era-years">About 200 years ago; temple registered in 1893 (2436 BE)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Evidence from the Samnakngan Noyobai Lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat Lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) states that the beginning of Wat Khiri Khet was a residence for forest monks used for ascetical practices, and it was about 200 years old when the database was created. Thus, a more exact year of construction should not yet be concluded. The name "Wat Lum" used by villagers reflects the low-lying terrain of the temple in the old market area.</p><p>Another set of local information sources states that the temple was built or registered on 15 May 1893 (2436 BE) and later used the name Wat Phra That Khiri Khet under the Maha Nikaya sect. Inside, there is an ancient pagoda base with a large banyan tree growing on the base, and there are Buddha relics serving as a center of faith.</p><p>In the picture of Takua Pa town during the tin mining and maritime trade era, the temple was located near the old market and the community where Hokkien Chinese and groups of miners came to settle. However, verifiable sources do not state whether this temple was built by a town ruler or any specific person, and no direct evidence connecting the builder to any Chinese group has been found. Therefore, it can be told cautiously that the temple served as a religious site and a spiritual anchor for the old town community, rather than guessing its founder.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning)</a> · <a href="https://andaman365.blogspot.com/2015/09/blog-post_21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andaman365 / Phangnga Channel</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>An old temple tied to the memories of the old town</h3><p class="era-years">1919 to 2020 (B.E. 2462 to 2563); the year of some events is still unknown</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>A verifiable milestone is that the temple received the royal granting of the land for the consecration boundary on 25 August 1919 (2462 BE), showing that the temple had stepped from a former practice area to a temple with a clear ecclesiastical status. Meanwhile, the community still called the temple "Wat Lum" or "Wat Talat Tai".</p><p>Along the way, the replica of the Buddha's footprint, built by laywoman Kui Limsakun and the Takua Pa Buddhist congregation, was once enshrined on Khao Phra Bat before being moved to be kept at Wat Senanut Rangsan, and then moved to be enshrined in the ordination hall of Wat Phra That Khiri Khet according to local data. This also made the temple serve as a keeper of the town's symbols of faith.</p><p>Municipal documents during 2020 (2563 BE) recorded that the temple had an ancient ordination hall alongside the new one, housing a replica of the Buddha's footprint, Pho Than Kaen Rak, and a jade Buddha statue. Additionally, there was the construction of the royal relic pagoda on Phra Bat mountain and the holding of a royal relic procession tradition on Makha Bucha Day. However, evidence confirming the years of each restoration, abandonment, archaeological site registration, or damage from the 2004 (2547 BE) tsunami has not yet been found.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://andaman365.blogspot.com/2015/09/blog-post_21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andaman365 / Phangnga Channel</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>Wat Lum remains a living temple of the old town</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The latest verifiable information shows that Wat Phrathat Khiri Khet is still a private Maha Nikaya temple and is listed among the religious sites of Thesaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality). Today, people still come to pay respects to the Buddha relics, the replica of the Buddha's footprint, Pho Than Kaen Rak, and the jade Buddha image, with both the old and new ordination halls coexisting in the area.</p><p>A contemporary activity with confirmed news is the 'Makha Bucha, Bowing the Heart to the Holy Relics' ceremony on 12 February 2025 (2568 BE). The holy relics were placed on a royal chariot, paraded around Takua Pa old town, and circumambulated around the vihara three times. This event reflects that the temple remains a center of faith and community activity, not just an old building.</p><p>No official announcements for 2026 have yet been found confirming opening hours, admission fees, individual caretakers, the latest structural condition, or temple-specific restoration projects. Therefore, visitors should check with the temple or municipality before traveling.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.thaipost.net/district-news/738702/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thai Post</a> · <a href="https://www.buddhaparisa.org/temple/23006/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">พุทธบริษัท ฐานข้อมูลวัดไทย (Buddhaparisa Thai Temple Database)</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">15 May 1893</p><h3>Temple Appears in Construction/Registration Records</h3><p>Local sources indicate 15 May 1893 (2436 BE) and the use of the name Wat Phrathat Khiri Khet.</p><a href="https://andaman365.blogspot.com/2015/09/blog-post_21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">25 August 1919</p><h3>Granted Royal Boundary</h3><p>Local sources indicate the temple was granted a royal boundary (Wisungkhammasima) and is affiliated with the Maha Nikaya sect.</p><a href="https://andaman365.blogspot.com/2015/09/blog-post_21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2020 (2563 BE)</p><h3>Recorded in the Old Town Plan</h3><p>The municipality recorded the old ordination hall, the new one, the replica footprint, Pho Than Kaen Rak, the jade Buddha image, and the relic procession tradition.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">12 February 2025</p><h3>Makha Bucha Relic Procession Around the Old Town</h3><p>A royal chariot procession carrying the sacred relics takes place around Takua Pa old town, along with a ceremony of circling the vihara three times.</p><a href="https://www.thaipost.net/district-news/738702/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Started as a Monastic Residence for Forest Monks</h3><p>Originally a residence for forest-dwelling monks to practice asceticism, dating back approximately 200 years according to official databases.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Replica Buddha Footprint Moved to the Temple</h3><p>The replica footprint was once on Khao Phra Bat (Phra Bat Hill), moved to Wat Senanuch Rangsan, and finally enshrined in the ordination hall of Wat Phrathat Khiri Khet.</p><a href="https://andaman365.blogspot.com/2015/09/blog-post_21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Ancient pagoda base and banyan tree</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>An ancient pagoda base with a large banyan tree growing on it.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Buddha relics</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>A major center of faith for the community, according to municipal data.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Replica of the Buddha's footprint</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Housed inside the temple.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Pho Than Kaen Rak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>A sacred figure noted by the municipality as a spot for worship within the temple.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Jade Buddha statue</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Located behind the main Buddha image, according to municipal documents.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2025-02-12 · Thai Post</p><h3>Phang Nga invited &#x27;Phra Si Borommathat&#x27; onto a royal chariot for a procession around Takua Pa old town</h3><p>The 2025 (2568 BE) Makha Bucha event was held at Wat Phrathat Khiri Khet, featuring a procession around the old town and circling the vihara.</p><a href="https://www.thaipost.net/district-news/738702/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The name 'Wat Lum' comes from the temple's low-lying terrain, not from its original official name, which is consistently used across all sources.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The ancient pagoda base with a large banyan tree growing on it is a prominent feature recorded by the cultural environment database.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Head towards Takua Pa old town, then enter Klan Kaeo Road / Khiri Khet Alley.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Travel to Takua Pa district first, then take a local vehicle or car into the old town area.</p><a href="https://www.lovethailand.org/travel/th/68-%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%B2/7959-%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%95-%28%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B8%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A1%29.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Specific distances or routes are not verified from the read sources.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/wat-khuha/">Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-pathum/">Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/">Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/">Wat Nikornwararam</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-sena/">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>Opening hours</summary><p>Not verified · Daily, 08:00 to 17:00</p></details><details><summary>Admission fee</summary><p>Not verified · Free admission</p></details><details><summary>How to dress</summary><p>Dress modestly when visiting religious sites</p></details><details><summary>Getting there from Takua Pa Town</summary><p>Head towards Takua Pa old town, then enter Klan Kaeo Road / Khiri Khet Alley.</p></details><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>Originally a forest monastery residence, it is about 200 years old. Locals call it 'Wat Lum' because it is located in a low-lying area.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>An ancient pagoda base with a large banyan tree growing on it. It is an important center of faith for the community according to municipality data. It is enshrined within the temple.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Samnakngan Nayobai Lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat Lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning): Art sites worthy of conservation</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality): Environmental Operation Plan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Visit Andaman Local: Takuapa destinations</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://visitandamanlocal.com/provinces/destinations/detail.php?id=95" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Love Thailand: Wat Phrathat Khiri Khet (Wat Lum)</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.lovethailand.org/travel/th/68-%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%B2/7959-%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%95-%28%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%Bัด%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B8%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A1%29.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4413" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Thai Post</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.thaipost.net/district-news/738702/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Andaman365 / Phangnga Channel</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://andaman365.blogspot.com/2015/09/blog-post_21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">พุทธบริษัท ฐานข้อมูลวัดไทย (Buddhaparisa Thai Temple Database)</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.buddhaparisa.org/temple/23006/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Patrick Le Petit photo album</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://patricklepetit.jalbum.net/PHANG%20NGA/PHOTOS/TAKUA%20PA/Takua%20Pa%20Old%20Town/Phratat%20Khiri%20Ket%20Chedi/indexb.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Phone numbers, Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts confirmed to belong to the temple are not verified.</li><li>The exact builder and founding year are not verified.</li><li>Direct evidence of the temple's role during the tin mining era is not verified.</li><li>Registration as a historical site for this specific temple is not verified; municipal documents only mention the registration of other temples.</li><li>Parking, restrooms, and wheelchair accessibility are not verified.</li><li>Reliable distances from Phuket, Khao Lak, the airport, or Takua Pa town are not verified.</li><li>Official image links with specified usage rights are not verified.</li><li>The exact year of construction and the name of the builder are not verified.</li><li>Direct evidence regarding ownership changes or periods of abandonment is not verified.</li><li>The years and details of individual renovations are not verified.</li><li>Evidence that the temple was affected by the 2004 tsunami is not verified.</li><li>A Fine Arts Department announcement confirming the temple's registration as a historical site is not verified.</li><li>Announcements or renovation projects specifically mentioning the temple in 2026 are not verified.</li><li>Old photographs with verifiable sources and rights are not verified.</li><li>Current opening hours, admission fees, and caretakers are not directly verified by the temple or municipality.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\water-trade\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Thung Tuek and the Takua Pa River Trade · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town&#x27;s fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Thung Tuek and the Takua Pa River Trade"><meta property="og:description" content="The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town&#x27;s fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Thung Tuek and the Takua Pa River, an Ancient Trade Route", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/water-trade/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Waterways and trade</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · River and Trade</span><h1>From Thung Tuk to the Takua Pa River: The Day the Waterway Built the Town</h1><p class="lead">The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town's fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation.</p><p class="story-meta">Updated 2026-09-07 · 7 min read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-port.webp" alt="ภาพเก่า เรือจอดที่ท่าน้ำตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Archive photograph: boats moored at the Takua Pa landing</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you stand by the Takua Pa River today, the slow and quiet water might seem like a mere backdrop to the old town landscape. However, looking back into history, this river never slept. It was the main artery that pumped prosperity, people, and resources into the area for centuries.</p><p>Takua Pa town was not located at its current coordinates from the beginning. It shifted and grew along the waterway, moving from the flatland near the estuary to the Talat Yai area, until the day river sediment forced the town centre to relocate once again.</p><p>The Takua Pa River is therefore not just a natural landscape, but the true author of history that brought global maritime commerce to the area, and the determiner of the town's destiny when the waterway became shallow.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">Thung Tuk, the Port Before the Town</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" alt="ตลาดริมน้ำตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takuapa Riverwalk</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>Before the image of Takua Pa town became the brick buildings and Sino-Portuguese commercial districts we see today, the original trade centre of this area was located at Thung Tuk, a flatland near the river mouth. Thung Tuk served as a bustling ancient trading port on the trans-peninsular route.</p><p>This estuary area did not grow in isolation. It was a crucial piece connecting to an extensive maritime trade network. It served as a stopover and goods exchange point for travellers of various nationalities, who relied on ocean currents and seasonal monsoons for navigation.</p><p>The Takua Pa waterway during that era was not solely for local transportation. It was used as a primary trans-peninsular route between the Andaman Sea and the Gulf of Thailand. With a favourable distance between the headwaters on both sides, this route drastically reduced travel time.</p><p>Importantly, using the river route helped navigators avoid the dangers and risks of sailing around the long Strait of Malacca. The waterway therefore acted as a bridge connecting two oceans, continuously bringing people, goods, and cultures into the area. This was the first chapter of the port town, before the centre of prosperity expanded deeper inland along the river.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Suggested, not settled status of the ancient port</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Takua Pa town was not located at its current coordinates from the beginning. It shifted and grew along the waterway.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The Steam Boiler and the Prosperity of Talat Yai</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" alt="สะพานเหล็กบุญสูง" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>As eras transitioned and maritime trade moved further inland, the town centre relocated to the Talat Yai community during the town's restoration in the reign of King Rama III. This area was surrounded by the river on three sides, making it highly suitable for both town defence and water transportation.</p><p>Takua Pa's water trade continued to flourish and left material traces visible to this day. If you travel to Wat Pathum Thara, or Wat Na Mueang (the town's front temple), in the Talat Yai area, you will find a large steam boiler on display. This metal piece is not mining machinery, but a crucial component of a historical steamboat.</p><p>Phraya Sena Nuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) partnered with Phraya Wichit Songkhram to purchase this steamboat in 1867 (2410 B.E.) to transport goods and suppress pirates in the waters. It serves as proof of the wealth and heavy traffic volume during that era.</p><p>Although the steamboat later deteriorated and sank in the Takua Pa River, the salvaged steam boiler still stands as a witness. The vibrancy of the Talat Yai community at that time was inextricably linked to the river. The people's way of life relied on the waterway, which functioned as the town's main street.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The temple's role in trade</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">The Day Sediment Accumulated and the Town Had to Move</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/wat-pathum.webp" alt="วัดปทุมธารา (วัดหน้าเมือง)" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>But the river is never static, and nature always plays a role in shaping the town's fate. Over time, sediment from the rapidly growing tin mining industry and natural phenomena accumulated until the Takua Pa River became increasingly shallow.</p><p>This geographical change directly impacted the town's economic structure. Navigation, once convenient, decreased and became more difficult. Large merchant ships that previously brought wealth could no longer dock as easily as before, causing the vibrancy of the Talat Yai community to decline.</p><p>Eventually, the town centre had to relocate from the original Talat Yai area to a new district called Yan Yao in 1913. This relocation was not caused by war or epidemics, but by the limitations of the changed waterway, which was no longer conducive to trade.</p><p>This historical event proves that the river had complete influence over human settlement. The day the water became shallow was the day the town had to pack its bags and move to breathe new life into an area better able to accommodate the changes.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Date and cause of the town relocation</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Boonsoong Iron Bridge, the Miners' Connection</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/thung-phra.webp" alt="สวนสาธารณะทุ่งพระโพธิ์" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Thung Phra Pho Park</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>As the town centre transitioned from the commercial port era to the golden age of tin mining, the riverside landscape and infrastructure were modified accordingly. In the past, the Takua Pa Canal was over forty metres wide, presenting a natural barrier that divided the area.</p><p>The width of the waterway meant miners and locals had to row boats back and forth between the old market side and the mining company's area. To solve this difficult commute, especially during the flood season when the current was swift, Mr Juti Boonsoong, founder of the tin dredging company, initiated the construction of a wooden bridge first, allowing people to walk and ride bicycles across.</p><p>Later in 1968, the bridge underwent a major renovation. Steel from decommissioned tin dredges was assembled to rebuild the Khok Khanun Iron Bridge, or as we familiarly know it, the Boonsoong Iron Bridge, spanning over two hundred metres across the river.</p><p>This iron bridge is therefore not just a construction object or an engineering structure. It is a shortcut that reduced travel time for workers, a goods transport route, and a connection for daily life. This landmark tells the story of the transition from the rowboat era to the mining industry era that powerfully sustained the town's economy.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Water Catchment and Merit-Making at Thung Phra Pho</h2><p>The Takua Pa River not only sustains the economy but is also tied to daily life and how locals cope with nature. Riverside areas like Thung Phra Pho Public Park are clear examples of adapting to coexist with the old town's water system.</p><p>Municipal planning documents indicate that the Thung Phra Pho area is important as water management infrastructure, with projects to dredge the canal and construct a pier. During times when the town faces flooding, this area is also used as an installation point for water pumps to alleviate floods for the community.</p><p>However, beyond the dimension of dealing with natural disasters, Thung Phra Pho is also an open space for cultural activities. On the full moon day of the twelfth lunar month every year, this wide riverside plaza is used as the venue for the municipality's annual Loy Krathong (November lantern-floating festival).</p><p>This diverse usage reflects that the riverside areas were not abandoned after the mining era. Instead, their functions were adapted to become both a town defence mechanism and an activity plaza that brings people together on merit-making days.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-5">Traces Still Flowing in the Present Landscape</h2><p>Today, although the mines have closed and large-scale maritime trade has become merely a past recorded on paper pages, many riverside locations are still alive and harmoniously coexist with the breath of the people in Takua Pa old town.</p><p>The Boonsoong Iron Bridge continues to serve as a community thoroughfare, where locals still walk and ride motorcycles back and forth. At the same time, it has become a landmark where people come to enjoy the view and take photos of the old dredge factory traces still visible from the bridge, or even being used as a filming location for foreign movies.</p><p>Along the canal near Wat Yan Yao (a local temple), a community market area named Takua Pa Riverside Market has been initiated to help revive the old atmosphere, allowing people to experience the riverside way of life once again. There are also seafood festivals held according to the cultural calendar.</p><p>The Takua Pa River continues to flow slowly. It is a guardian of memories from the days when it was a global port town crowded with merchant ships, to the day it became the tranquil viewpoint of the old town we see today.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="iron-bridge">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="wat-pathum">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Thung Phra Pho Park</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="thung-phra">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Architectural characteristics and town layout details of the Thung Tuk port in the era prior to the tin mining trade</li><li>Contemporary photographs with clear usage rights depicting the congestion of water traffic during the era when steamboats were still operating</li><li>Documents officially recording the day of the relocation to Yan Yao, and the voices of the villagers in the Talat Yai community at that time</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Thung Tuek Ancient Trading Port</cite> · กรมศิลปากร (Fine Arts Department) · Thung Tuek archaeological site article · <a href="https://www.finearts.go.th/architecture/view/35708-ทุ่งตึกเมืองท่าการค้าโบราณ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town Documents</cite> · สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · PDF pages 11-13, 55-56, 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Archaeological Site Database: Talat Yai Community</cite> · ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Community historical data · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Bun Sung Iron Bridge (Khok Khanun)</cite> · ฐานข้อมูลท้องถิ่นภาคใต้ มหาวิทยาลัยสงขลานครินทร์ (Southern Local Database, Prince of Songkla University) · History and overview page · <a href="https://clib.psu.ac.th/southerninfo/content/1/de645890" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Environmental Action Plan Document 2022 (2565 BE)</cite> · Thetsaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Project Records and Water Management · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_strategy/2022-12_231dcd9d5169573.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Flood News and Water Pump Installation</cite> · MGR Online · Regional News · <a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9560000096203" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Riverside Market, Ruamjai Pracharath</cite> · Krom Kan Kha Phainai Krasuang Phanit (Department of Internal Trade, Ministry of Commerce) · Must-Visit Market Information · <span class="source-offline">View sources <small>แหล่งเดิมออฟไลน์ · ตรวจเมื่อ 8 ก.ย. 2569</small></span> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Morning News: Boon Sung Iron Bridge</cite> · NBT2HD · News Program 30 January 2026 (2569) · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QUZ54DTc0LQ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Seafood Festival Calendar, Takua Pa Riverside Market</cite> · Krasuang Watthanatham (Ministry of Culture) · Cultural Activity Calendar · <a href="https://calendar.m-culture.go.th/events/103651" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Trans-peninsular distance and the origin of Thung Tuk</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The distance figures are data from the ONEP report, and the establishment of Thung Tuk refers to the Fine Arts Department's explanation. The website maintains the level of the text according to the original documents and has not verified new conclusions. <a href="https://www.finearts.go.th/architecture/view/35708-ทุ่งตึกเมืองท่าการค้าโบราณ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Town relocation due to the shallow river</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The date and reason for the town's relocation in 1913 are compiled data based on the ONEP report, which should be cited from the original source report when used in academic work. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Origin of the steam boiler at Wat Na Mueang</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Information from the Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre database indicates the purchase of the steamboat in 1867, but this piece of evidence does not verify that the temple operated the mining business directly. <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">Thung Tuk, the Port Before the Town</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The Steam Boiler and the Prosperity of Talat Yai</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">The Day Sediment Accumulated and the Town Had to Move</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Boonsoong Iron Bridge, the Miners' Connection</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Water Catchment and Merit-Making at Thung Phra Pho</a></li><li><a href="#section-5">Traces Still Flowing in the Present Landscape</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
