t><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/city/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/city/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Know the city</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · About the Town</span><h1>Takua Pa: The Town That Moved with the River</h1><p class="lead">From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered.</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2 is-illustration"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" alt="ตลาดริมน้ำตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Takuapa Riverwalk</span><span class="credit">Illustration · No licensed photo available for this subject yet</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you have a chance to take a walk in Takua Pa old town on a holiday afternoon, you will see old houses, long streets, and a quiet, charming atmosphere. But deep beneath the cracks in the old walls and the slow-flowing river, this town hides a story of major transformation.</p><p>Takua Pa was not located here from the beginning, and it did not always look like this. The town is a living entity that grew, expanded, and moved in rhythm with its resources and the river. From a stopover point on the trans-peninsular route, it became a bustling major market town, and later a town that had to find a new home when the river was no longer favorable.</p><p>We will take you to read Takua Pa through what remains: the governor's residence wall that tells a story of wealth, the old temple by the canal, and the river that was once its main artery. This is a town with more than one version of history.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Ongoing Debate Over the Town's Name</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/wat-kongkha.webp" alt="วัดคงคาภิมุข (วัดคงคา)" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>When speaking of Takua Pa, the first question many people wonder is where the town's name came from. Official sources and historians still offer different explanations, and to date, there is no evidence that can bring this to a single, settled conclusion.</p><p>The first explanation links the town's name to the word "Takola", which appears in ancient records as a major port on the trans-peninsular route. This word is interpreted as a possible root that gradually changed in pronunciation to the current name over several centuries.</p><p>Another explanation is based on local geography and resources. It suggests that the name comes from combining the word "Takua" (which means tin, a mineral abundantly found in the area) and "Pa" (which means forest, reflecting the rich natural environment surrounding the town).</p><p>Although documents from the Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP) list both explanations as basic information, they clearly emphasize that there is no definitive evidence to confirm either one. The town's name remains the first mystery greeting visitors, leaving us to imagine the past through different explanations.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The origin of the town's name is not settled</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Takua Pa was not located here from the beginning, and it did not always look like this. The town is a living entity that grew, expanded, and moved in rhythm with its resources and the river.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The Governor's Residence Wall and Town Defense</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" alt="กำแพงจวนเจ้าเมืองตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Governor’s Residence Wall</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The image of Takua Pa's past wealth is inextricably linked to the tin mining business. One of the structures that most clearly reflects the power and context of that era is the Takua Pa governor's residence wall, which stands as a historical witness to this day.</p><p>Scholars have suggested, not settled, that this wall was built during the time of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) while he was the governor between 1840 (2383 BE) and 1881. The wall surrounding the residence was designed as a defensive camp. During an era when the town faced conflicts among Chinese groups linked to the massive benefits from tin mining, the wall served as both a home for the ruler and a fortress to maintain peace.</p><p>The wall's structure is highly stable, constructed with layers of mortar mixed with gravel and sand. The base was designed to be wider than the wall itself. The parts that remain intact today reach a height of about 5 meters. Inside the wall, the area was divided into the front palace, the main hall, and the rear palace, with connecting doors well-proportioned throughout.</p><p>The significance of this governor's residence also appears in historical records when King Chulalongkorn (Rama V) came to inspect the residence on May 28, 1890 (109 RS or 2433 BE). During the excavation by the Fine Arts Department in 1999, Chinese ceramics from the Qing Dynasty and European ceramics were found, providing evidence of Takua Pa's international trade connections.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">The construction year is suggested, not settled, based on the governor's tenure</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">A Town That Grew with the River</h2><p>The Takua Pa River is not just a backdrop for the town, but the main artery that has nourished life and trade since the pioneering era. Official documents explain that Takua Pa old town is an ancient settlement located by the river, and it prospered as a trade center on the trans-peninsular route.</p><p>The bustling water trade and tin mining led to the expansion of an area called Talat Yai (the big market) during the reign of King Rama III, which became an important economic center. Near the hustle and bustle of trade, Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the town's temple) or Wat Kongkha, is situated on the west bank of the Takua Pa canal, serving as the spiritual center for the townspeople.</p><p>The history of Wat Kongkha reflects the complexity of the old town's information. The cultural environment data states that this temple was built in 1762, while the archaeological database uses 1936 as the construction year for the beautiful southern style ordination hall. This hall is decorated with stucco floral patterns and Buddha images, which are still preserved today.</p><p>The differences in these sets of numbers are something we often find in local history. Explaining the town's antiquity through official documents only provides an overview of the settlement in the entire area. It does not mean that every building, every bridge, or every location in the present town has the same age dating back to the town's founding era.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">The age of the location and the age of the building might not be the same year</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">The Day the River Grew Shallow and the Town Had to Move</h2><p>But nature never stays still, and the river is always ready to change. As time passed, the river that built the town began to change its role. ONEP reports note the end of the golden age of Talat Yai, which was primarily caused by geography and changes in resources.</p><p>Long and continuous tin mining caused a massive amount of sand and sediment to flow down and accumulate in the river. The Takua Pa River, which used to be deep enough for cargo ships and passenger boats to navigate easily, gradually became shallower until large-scale navigation declined and became difficult.</p><p>Eventually, when the river was no longer favorable as a center for trade and transportation, moving the town's administrative center was inevitable. Official reports state that Takua Pa had to move its town center to an area called Yan Yao in 1913.</p><p>That relocation marked the end of Talat Yai's prosperity as a center, turning this area into an old town stopped in time with the past. It left behind Sino-Portuguese architecture and traces of development for future generations to experience and study.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">Reasons for the town relocation are based on ONEP reports</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Reading the Town Today</h2><p>Today, Takua Pa old town has come back to life in a different form from the past. The waterfront by the canal, which used to be a port for transporting tin, has now been revived into the area of the Takua Pa riverside market. It becomes lively again from Saturday afternoon to evening, filled with smiles, the aroma of local food, and the chatter of locals.</p><p>The governor's residence wall, once strong, is no longer used to defend against enemies. Instead, it has become a public space open to visitors and is often used as a venue for traditional festivals or major town events. Meanwhile, Wat Kongkha Phimuk continues to function as an active temple with residing monks. It is a peaceful place for meditation and a historical site that is still breathing.</p><p>If you have the chance to walk along the cultural street and the river, what surrounds you are not just stagnant ruins of the past, but evidence of adaptation for survival. The town that moved with the river back then continues to flow and change along with the people today.</p><p>Before fully believing any numbers or legends of the town, we would like to invite you to read and observe this town carefully. The history of Takua Pa is a charming large book. Some pages might be missing due to time, and some pages are still being written by all of us.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="wat-kongkha">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Governor’s Residence Wall</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="governor-wall">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact construction year of the governor's residence wall</li><li>Definitive evidence confirming the origin of Takua Pa's name</li><li>The reasons and builders of Wat Kongkha in its early days</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town</cite> · สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Takua Pa Old Town webpage · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town Documents</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) · PDF pages 11-13, 55-56, 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Archaeological Site Database: Takua Pa Town</cite> · ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · List of archaeological sites and suggested city walls · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">The town's name originated from 'Takola' or 'Takua and Pa'</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">ONEP documents list both explanations, but emphasize that there is no definitive evidence to confirm either one. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The governor's residence wall was built between 1840 and 1881</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is suggested, not settled, based on the tenure of Governor Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon), and is not an exact year of construction. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Wat Kongkha Phimuk was built in 1762</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">The cultural environment data states 1762, while the archaeological database uses 1936 as the construction year for the ordination hall. The age of the location and the remaining building might not align. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Ongoing Debate Over the Town's Name</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The Governor's Residence Wall and Town Defense</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">A Town That Grew with the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">The Day the River Grew Shallow and the Town Had to Move</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Reading the Town Today</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Stories of Takua Pa: History, Tin and the River · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Long reads on how Takua Pa grew: the river that moved the town, buildings paid for with tin money, Peranakan food, and the old town waking up again."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Stories of Takua Pa: History, Tin and the River"><meta property="og:description" content="Long reads on how Takua Pa grew: the river that moved the town, buildings paid for with tin money, Peranakan food, and the old town waking up again."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Takua Pa Stories", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "ItemList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 4, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 5, "url": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="stories-index-v2"><div class="intro"><a class="eyebrow" href="/en/">Takua Pa 101 / Old Town Guide</a><h1>Takua Pa Stories</h1><p class="lead">Four perspectives for reading the town through its sources · Getting to know the town, old houses and architecture, waterways and trade, people and flavors</p></div><div class="story-grid-v2"><article class="story-card-v2 story-card-v2-lead"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">Know the city</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/city/">Takua Pa: The Town That Moved with the River</a></h2><p>From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered.</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">6 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article><article class="story-card-v2"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">Old houses and architecture</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/architecture/">Buildings built with tin money and dredger steel · Architecture that remembers the town</a></h2><p>Read Takua Pa through the shophouses on Culture Street, Boon Sung Iron Bridge, the governor's residence, and Tao Ming School · The transition embedded in brick, mortar, and steel structures</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">6 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article><article class="story-card-v2"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">Waterways and trade</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/water-trade/">From Thung Tuk to the Takua Pa River: The Day the Waterway Built the Town</a></h2><p>The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town's fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation.</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">7 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article><article class="story-card-v2"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/food-center.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">People and flavors</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/food-people/">Flavours of the Baba Community and the Old Market</a></h2><p>Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Street</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">7 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article><article class="story-card-v2"><div class="card-art-v2"><img src="/assets/tao-ming.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></div><div class="card-content-v2"><span class="chapter">City topics</span><h2><a href="/en/stories/kuapapoh/">Kuapapoh: The Day Old Houses Stand Up to Tell Their Stories</a></h2><p>A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover</p><div class="card-meta-v2"><span class="story-mins">6 minutes</span><span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Open this story ↗</span></div></div></article></div><aside class="source"><h2>How to read this page</h2><p>This article was compiled from official documents and academic-institution databases, with the evidence level identified for each topic · Check shop details, opening hours, and schedules with providers before travelling</p></aside></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\kuapapoh\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened"><meta property="og:description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Kuapa Poh, an Old Takua Pa House Brought Back to Life", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/kuapapoh/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>City topics</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · Town Topics</span><h1>Kuapapoh: The Day Old Houses Stand Up to Tell Their Stories</h1><p class="lead">A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" alt="กั่วป่าโพ้ · แบรนด์ของโครงการเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Kuapapoh, the identity of the old town project</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>Imagine strolling along Sri Takua Pa Road, a street lined with old shophouses that have stood the test of time. Many of these buildings have their wooden doors quietly shut, sleeping away alongside the long-gone golden age of tin mining. Underneath that quietness, however, lie countless memories of the town, its people, and its trade, waiting to be told.</p><p>In recent years, a creative space project called 'Kuapapoh Ji Show Mueang' has stepped in to open the doors of these once-abandoned old houses and bring them back to life. They did not just repaint or repair the roofs, but are bringing the stories of Takua Pa back on display for the newer generations to see.</p><p>This movement reflects a town trying to stand up and tell its own story through the perspectives of locals, artists, and community collaboration. However, in reading these changes, we must still distinguish between newly created narratives for tourism and historical facts that still need careful verification.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/shophouse-109.webp" alt="ตึกแถวเลขที่ 109 บนถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 109 on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>We often remember Takua Pa through its prosperous tin mining era. When that industry declined, many buildings were left behind. One of them is house number 78, formerly a mine owner's residence and a tin mining company office. Today, this once-empty space has been transformed into the 'Kuapa Mueang Museum', exhibiting mining equipment, old maps, and important documents for visitors to experience the traces of the past.</p><p>Not far away on the same route is house number 109, the building of the Kang Tang Yui Kuan or Wui Kun shrine, an association of the Hakka Chinese. The second floor of the building still houses the deity Tham Kung Ya for people to pay their respects. But the ground floor space, which used to be empty and unused, has been turned into the 'Kuapapoh Gallery' to host exhibitions and showcase the wisdom of the Takua Pa old town residents.</p><p>Another significant building that the project has dusted off is building number 305, or 'Tao Ming School' (the town's first Chinese school), which was an educational center during the prosperous mining era. The team came up with the idea to revive this space under the name 'Tao Ming: The Spark of Learning', using it as a venue to exhibit knowledge on architecture, culture, and traditions related to Takua Pa.</p><p>The effort to bring these three spaces back into use is an initiative filled with the project owners' determination to add economic value and create worth for the town. We must collectively understand, however, that this is an explanation and operation at the community project level, not an official declaration of historical site status or historical endorsement from government agencies.</p></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Working with community narratives is about balancing beautiful memories with facts that must be verified.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" alt="อาคารโรงเรียนเต้าหมิง ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>As soon as you hear the name 'Kuapapoh', the first question that usually follows is where this word comes from. The project's archival information explains that this was the term Hokkien Chinese used in the past to call Takua Pa town, dividing the translation into two parts: 'Kuapa' meaning Takua Pa, and 'Poh' meaning market or port.</p><p>When combined, the word conveys 'Takua Pa port market', reflecting the past image of Chinese people sailing across the sea to trade and mine tin, using the pier as a center for exchanging both goods and culture. In addition, in the community's belief, the word Kuapapoh is also embedded in the rituals performed at various shrines during the vegetarian festival, becoming a word with deep meaning in the hearts of the townspeople.</p><p>While this is a beautiful story that connects well with the community's roots, in academic circles, the origin of the Takua Pa town name is still debated with several suggested, not settled hypotheses. Some refer to the ancient name Takola, while others base it on the words takua (lead) and pa (forest).</p><p>Therefore, the project's explanation is considered a record of memory and interpretation through the context of the Hokkien Chinese community, rather than a single, settled historical academic conclusion. This is the reason we must read this story with an open mind and caution.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">not settled academically</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/tao-ming.webp" alt="โรงเรียนเต้าหมิง" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The liveliness of the old town district reappeared concretely in September 2026 (2569 BE) when 78 Studio on Takua Pa Culture Street opened its space to host a solo art exhibition titled 'Peranakan Charm · Still Becoming'.</p><p>The artist behind these works is Tinnaphop Ngansathir, or Chang Pong. He is a native of Takua Pa old market, growing up with cartoon drawings, superheroes, and Chinese deities familiar in the local culture. After accumulating artistic experience for a long time, he chose to use his brush tip to convey the stories of the town he is attached to.</p><p>The artworks in the exhibition focus on extracting Peranakan colors, patterns, and clothing for display, serving as a bridge connecting the glorious past with people in the present era. Each painting is not just visually beautiful, but an emotional record of the artist towards the town.</p><p>Even though the project documents clearly record the exhibition hosting and the opening of space for artists to express themselves, what we still do not see in the reports are the number of attendees or a formal economic impact assessment. These activities thus serve as cultural ripples rather than official government statistics.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" alt="ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันที่ยังเห็นได้ในเมืองเก่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Peranakan tile patterns still visible in the old town</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><p>To help visitors understand the identity of Takua Pa old town more deeply, the project did not stop at organizing exhibitions. They also compiled and presented the '6 Or' concept to use as a framework for viewing the town's heritage hidden in daily life.</p><p>This concept starts with 'Aphon' (Apparel) or local clothing, whether it is lace fabrics, batik sarongs, and various accessories. Followed by 'Asai' (Abode) which covers the way of life, trading, and living in accordance with the times. As for 'Ahan' (Food), it is indispensable. It is the traditional flavors passed down from generation to generation, and the old restaurants that still keep their stoves on to this day.</p><p>In the dimension of the mind, the town is driven by 'Arom' (Emotion) expressed through art, music, and the sound of drums in the vegetarian festival. Meanwhile, 'Aram' (Temples and Shrines) is the center of faith, including temples, shrines, and Yui Kuan scattered around the old town district. And finally, 'Akhan' (Architecture), the structures that stand as witnesses to change.</p><p>All these concepts were also further developed in the design of souvenirs, such as the Poh Shop project's t-shirts. The revenue from the sales is brought back to fund the restoration and drive the town's activities forward, truly circulating value back to the community.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" alt="ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Kuapapoh project allows us to see the determination of the new generation and locals who want to preserve and build upon the stories of Takua Pa. They created creative spaces, brought in art as a medium, and turned once-closed houses into new travel destinations.</p><p>What is recorded on the project's website and documents serves as a contemporary archive that the community chose to tell about itself. It is a memory full of life and the pride of the residents.</p><p>At the same time, however, reading these stories also calls for us to maintain a balance between appreciation and historical facts. Many parts of the information are explanations that have not been endorsed by government agencies and cannot replace calendars or academic documents.</p><p>This is the challenge and fun of walking around Takua Pa old town today. We have the task of listening to those beautiful stories while simultaneously asking questions and finding answers from reliable sources, so that our journey does not end with just looking at pictures, but truly understanding the town in all dimensions.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Tao Ming School</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The number of attendees or the official economic impact generated by the project</li><li>The legal status or endorsement from government agencies of the three houses</li><li>The clear and settled origin of the Takua Pa town name in history</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Kuapapoh · Project Archives</cite> · Kuapapoh Project · content.json · origin, events, openings sections · <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Defining houses number 78, 109, and 305 as creative spaces</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is an explanation from the project owner, not a declaration of historical site or space status from a government agency. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The origin of the word Kuapapoh translates to Takua Pa port market</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The origin of the Takua Pa town name in historical work still has several suggested, not settled hypotheses, thus the project's explanation should not be used as a settled academic conclusion. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The hosting of the Peranakan Charm exhibition by Tinnaphop Ngansathir in September 2026 (2569 BE)</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Referenced from project data, but not a retrospective official calendar confirming attendee numbers or economic impact. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div><section class="research-section" id="project-gallery"><h2>Images from Kuapapoh Project Archive</h2><div data-gallery><div class="gallery-tabs" hidden><button type="button" data-tab="today">Present photos</button><button type="button" data-tab="archive">Historical photos</button></div><div id="gallery-today" data-panel="today"><h3>Present photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · กั่วป่าโพ้ แบรนด์เมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" alt="กั่วป่าโพ้ แบรนด์เมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Town Brand<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันเมืองเก่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" alt="ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันเมืองเก่า" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Old Town Peranakan Tile Patterns<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div><div id="gallery-archive" data-panel="archive"><h3>Historical photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · แผ่นเล่าประวัติกั่วป่าโพ้"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" alt="แผ่นเล่าประวัติกั่วป่าโพ้" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh History Board<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · หวุ่ยกุ่น"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" alt="หวุ่ยกุ่น" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Wui Kun<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 1"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" alt="เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 1" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 1<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" alt="เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 2" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 2<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div></div></section></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua P